@capability-s-space-1-any-viewer-transient-mutate
@no-oracle-s-space-1-any-viewer-transient-state-lane-semantics
@comparison-ordered-json-v1
@mutations-s-space-1-any-viewer-transient
Feature: Fold every committed directory batch and presence vector of s.space.space's 👁️viewer/🫧️transient lane
  `SpaceIndexTransientMutation` is the transient lane of the Space index (owned by its read-only viewer, reused by its
  editor): it folds bounded batches of one space's directory events into the Space table's projection through the canonical
  kernel fold, and replaces per-artifact presence rows. The projection is derived hub state — never persisted, shared or
  undone — so both verbs, `apply-directory-events` and `set-artifact-presence`, are non-invertible by declaration, and each
  batch is one bounded item priced by the batch, never by the space's history. It is this repository's own state record, so
  the case records the no-oracle decision `s-space-1-any-viewer-transient-state-lane-semantics` in
  `👁️viewer/🫧️transient/🔮️oracles/🔣️.json` and asserts every law inside the subject handlers through the shared
  `semio_s_plugin_stdio_test_oracle::law::vector` module: the applied snapshot is the committed after-snapshot, the produced
  delta is the committed `🔺️diff`, the diagnostics are the ones the committed `🎯️outcome` declares, and only an applied
  vector moves the snapshot. Production dispatch is reached through `space_index_transient_mutation_report_json`, which runs
  `Mutation::diff(..).apply_to` exactly as the transient store does.

  Both kinds are non-invertible, so their inverse law is asserted where it is meaningful: a verb that changes nothing (a
  replayed history, a refused race, an equal or refused presence row) leaves nothing to undo — the kind computes no inverse
  step and the projection stays the committed before-snapshot. A folded batch is never undone; the host replays the space's
  history from its origin instead.

  @id-mutate
  @level-exhaustive
  @mode-conformance
  Scenario Outline: Fold <id> and land on the committed after-snapshot, diff and outcome
    Given the committed <id> vector under 👁️viewer/🫧️transient/🧫️fixtures
    When its verb is folded into that vector's before-snapshot through space_index_transient_mutation_report_json
    Then the folded snapshot, the produced diff and the diagnostics are exactly what the vector commits
    Examples:
      | id |
      | apply-directory-events-applied |
      | apply-directory-events-no-op |
      | apply-directory-events-rejected |
      | set-artifact-presence-applied |
      | set-artifact-presence-no-op |
      | set-artifact-presence-rejected |

  @id-inverse
  @level-exhaustive
  @mode-property
  Scenario Outline: Undoing <id> leaves the committed before-snapshot
    Given the committed <id> vector under 👁️viewer/🫧️transient/🧫️fixtures
    When its verb is folded into that vector's before-snapshot through space_index_transient_mutation_report_json
    Then the kind computes no inverse step and the projection is exactly the committed before-snapshot
    Examples:
      | id |
      | apply-directory-events-no-op |
      | apply-directory-events-rejected |
      | set-artifact-presence-no-op |
      | set-artifact-presence-rejected |
